# Database migrations

Alembic manages versioned changes to the PostgreSQL schema. SQLAlchemy models
describe the schema the application expects, while Alembic migration scripts
describe how to move an existing database from one schema version to another.

## Generated files

- `../alembic.ini` is the main Alembic configuration file at the repository
  root. It identifies the migration directory and configures logging. Database
  credentials must not be stored in this file; `env.py` will read them from
  environment variables.
- `env.py` is Alembic's runtime entry point. It will build the database
  connection and expose the SQLAlchemy `Base.metadata` registry so Alembic can
  compare the models with the real database.
- `script.py.mako` is the template Alembic uses when generating a new revision
  file. It supplies the revision identifiers and the `upgrade()` and
  `downgrade()` function structure.
- `versions/` stores the ordered migration scripts. Each script represents one
  reviewed schema change and links to its parent revision.
- `README` is this guide. It documents the purpose and safe use of the migration
  setup.

After the first migration is applied, PostgreSQL will also contain an
`alembic_version` table. Alembic uses that table to record the revision applied
to that particular database.

## Intended workflow

1. Change a SQLAlchemy model.
2. Generate a candidate migration with `alembic revision --autogenerate`.
3. Read the generated `upgrade()` and `downgrade()` functions; autogeneration
   is assistance, not approval.
4. Apply the reviewed migration with `alembic upgrade head`.
5. Run application tests against the migrated schema.

Run Alembic through the project environment and load local configuration with
`uv run --env-file .env alembic ...`.

Never commit database passwords or other secrets to Alembic configuration.
The connection and model-metadata wiring in `env.py` must be completed before
the first migration is generated.
